sum='Albert Einstein was born on 14 March 1879 in Ulm, Germany. His mother thought he was a freak because his head seemed too large; he did not talk till two and a half, and his playmates called him “Brother Boring” as he preferred to play alone. A headmaster said he would “never make a success at anything”. He learnt the violin at six and became a gifted amateur violinist. At 15 he felt stifled by the strict school in Munich and left it for good; he studied in liberal Switzerland (Zurich), where he met Mileva Maric, a fellow student – his ally against the professors; his mother did not approve of her. In 1900 he graduated but was unemployed; he worked as a technical expert in the patent office in Bern and secretly developed his own ideas – he called his desk drawer the “bureau of theoretical physics”. In 1905 he published the Special Theory of Relativity and the famous equation E = mc². He married Mileva in 1903; they divorced in 1919. The General Theory of Relativity (1915) was confirmed in 1919 and he became famous; he won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921. When the Nazis came to power (1933) he emigrated to the USA. In 1939 he wrote to President Franklin Roosevelt warning that Germany might make an atom bomb – the letter started the arms race. He was shaken by the bombing of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, wrote to the United Nations proposing a world government, and campaigned for peace and democracy. He died in 1955 at 76 – remembered as a visionary and world citizen. Poem: W.B. Yeats longs to go to Innisfree, build a small cabin of clay and wattles, grow nine bean-rows, keep a beehive and live alone in the peaceful, bee-loud glade; even on grey city pavements he hears the lake water lapping in his “deep heart’s core”.',
